The '''Pastry War''' ({{lang-bl|Guerra de Pastisceri}}), was a year long conflict that pit the largest [[Communities of Balisca|Baliscan republics]]—[[Bayara]] and [[Hesperia]]—against each other, and threatened to split the [[Confederation of the Southern Provinces|fledgling Southern Confederation]]. The war followed longstanding intercommunal tensions between predominately Catalan-speaking Cathars and Aragonese-speaking Catholics in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]], and disputes regarding the [[Confederal Question|extent of confederal power]]. Conflict boiled over following the murder of an Catalan [[W:Pastry|pastry]] chef in [[Xàbia]] by a Catholic mob, resulting in escalating reprisals and counter-reprisals which eventually culminated in [[Confederal emergency|confederal intervention]]. 

It was ultimately ended by the [[Treaty of Navassa]].

The Treaty was largely viewed as illegitimate by Bayarans (who denounced it as unacceptably favorable to the Catalans) and Estuarians (who were frustrated by it's failure to secure their rights) alike, and became increasingly hard to enforce. Further conflict was initially avoided with the establishment of the [[Cerdanyan Legion]] which administered the Estuarian territories. The Legion was withdrawn in 1826, and the Confederation ultimately descended into [[Baliscan Civil War|civil war]] and collapsed in 1828 following Bayara's renunciation of the [[Article of Confederation]]. 

